## Experiment L (v1.1): what changed after the first submission

Three model-layer decisions, each by a rule written before it ran (`docs/experiments/L-final.md`), on the train-carved validation split, the holdout
read once: (1) **the stated touristiness preference steers the served ranking** through a scoring-layer factor `pref_align^gamma` (no retraining), with gamma
and the MMR lambda selected together; (2) **the MMR lambda was selected**, not defaulted (selected: {{longtail_stages.lambda_default}}, which turns the diversity
re-rank off and sits on the pre-registered entropy constraint); (3) **the retrieval design was re-decided** on validation with the ranker retrained per design, after
Gate-B's blocking rows were amended (long-tail recall and a 300-candidate serving budget block; the a-priori overall-recall and chance-lift targets report only): the
learned retriever stays. On the holdout the scorecard's MET rows are unchanged (seven MET rows before, eight now: scenario-4 overlap
{{l_pre_scenarios.diagnostic_vs_base_jaccard|.3f}} -> {{scenarios.overlap_matrix.diagnostic_vs_base_jaccard|.3f}} passes its target); one MISSED row got worse (confidence-decile Spearman
{{confidence_diagnosis.post_fix.decile_spearman_recomputed|.3f}} -> {{confidence_decile_validation.spearman_rho|.3f}}).

## Anticipated questions

**Why does the ranking barely change when the touristiness preference flips?** In the ranker alone it did, and we measured why (`docs/TECHNICAL.md` section 3.1; `results/parts/touristiness_axis.json`):
outcomes carry the preference (trip-level Spearman {{touristiness_axis.c_d_localness_gradient_by_stated_pref.all.label_pref_vs_rho_spearman|+.2f}}) but the ranker reproduced
{{l_holdout_report.raw_ranker_gradient_reproduction_all_candidate_rows.cold_start.score_over_label|.0%}} of that gradient for cold-start trips and {{l_holdout_report.raw_ranker_gradient_reproduction_all_candidate_rows.with_history.score_over_label|.0%}} for trips with history, because history-derived features are the cheaper split
(implicit-taste attribution {{h_experiments.h0a_shap_shares_final_model.implicit_taste|.1%}}; removing the raw implicit block even improves holdout NDCG@10 by {{ablations.6.delta_ndcg@10|+.4f}}). That is our
measured answer to the brief's explicit-versus-implicit question: the blend is learned and, with history, the implicit side won. A stated "prefer less touristy" is a per-trip instruction, so it now enters
the scoring layer (v1.1): on the real holdout trips the served top-10 flip-overlap falls {{l_holdout_report.shipped_config.flip_overlap|.3f}} -> {{l_holdout_report.selected_config.flip_overlap|.3f}} (with history {{l_holdout_report.shipped_config.flip_overlap_with_history|.3f}} -> {{l_holdout_report.selected_config.flip_overlap_with_history|.3f}}), the
scenario-4 overlap is {{scenarios.overlap_matrix.diagnostic_vs_base_jaccard|.3f}} against a target of at most 0.35, and served NDCG@10 does not fall ({{l_holdout_report.shipped_config.served_ndcg10_mean|.4f}} -> {{l_holdout_report.selected_config.served_ndcg10_mean|.4f}}). Two honest caveats:
the factor **overshoots** the outcome gradient (about {{l_holdout_report.selected_config.gradient_reproduction_of_utility.with_history.score_over_label|.0f}}x), because the pre-registered rule minimised flip-overlap under two constraints rather than matching the slope; and the selection turned the diversity re-rank off.

**You shipped the retriever even though the legacy six-channel union scores higher end-to-end.** DR12 reports it: on the *holdout* the legacy union reaches NDCG@10
{{decision_register.rows.11.results.ndcg10_end_to_end.legacy_six_channel_retrained_ranker|.4f}} against {{decision_register.rows.11.results.ndcg10_end_to_end.learned_retriever_shipped_ranker|.4f}} (paired p={{decision_register.rows.11.results.ndcg10_end_to_end.paired_wilcoxon_p|.4f}}),
with recall {{decision_register.rows.11.results.candidate_sets.learned_retriever.recall_overall|.3f}} against {{decision_register.rows.11.results.candidate_sets.legacy_six_channel.recall_overall|.3f}} and long-tail recall
{{decision_register.rows.11.results.candidate_sets.learned_retriever.recall_long_tail|.3f}} against {{decision_register.rows.11.results.candidate_sets.legacy_six_channel.recall_long_tail|.3f}}. A holdout comparison cannot drive a switch, so v1.1 re-decided the design on validation with a
rule fixed in advance (`docs/experiments/L-final.md`): three designs (learned, legacy, their union), the ranker retrained per design, four seeds, blocking constraints of long-tail recall at least 0.75 and at most
300 effective candidates, and a +0.010 adoption bar. Result: learned {{l3b_designs.designs.A.v_ndcg10|.4f}}, legacy {{l3b_designs.designs.B.v_ndcg10|.4f}} (long-tail recall {{l3b_designs.designs.B.long_tail_recall|.3f}}, below the brief's requirement),
union {{l3b_designs.designs.C.v_ndcg10|.4f}} ({{l3b_designs.designs.C.effective_k|.0f}} candidates, over the serving budget). Nothing cleared the bar, so the learned retriever stays; on validation the legacy union is not ahead, so its holdout
advantage in DR12 is not a stable property of the design.

**Long-tail precision is {{longtail.precision|.3f}} against a 0.40 target.** The 0.40 was set a priori, without reference to the data: the candidate pool's own long-tail positive rate is
{{derived.longtail_lift_over_base_rate.base_rate|.3f}}, so the served list is a {{derived.longtail_lift_over_base_rate.final_after_mmr|.2f}}x lift over the base rate (precision was {{l_holdout_report.shipped_config.longtail_precision|.3f}} before v1.1) and the raw ranker's top-10 a {{derived.longtail_lift_over_base_rate.raw_ranker|.2f}}x lift (precision {{longtail_stages.stages.1_raw_ranker_top10.long_tail_precision|.3f}}). The E3 stage table shows where it goes: the hard gate takes it to
{{longtail_stages.stages.2_after_hard_gate_raw_order.long_tail_precision|.3f}}, the utility (now with the preference factor) leaves it at {{longtail_stages.stages.3_after_utility.long_tail_precision|.3f}} while the long-tail share rises to {{longtail_stages.stages.3_after_utility.long_tail_share|.3f}}
(target 0.25), and MMR at the selected lambda {{longtail_stages.lambda_default}} changes nothing. The post-hoc lambda curve (a diagnostic, not a selection) runs from {{derived.mmr_lift_lambda_0_5|.2f}}x at lambda 0.5 to
{{derived.mmr_lift_lambda_1_0|.2f}}x at lambda 1, so the target is out of reach even without a diversity term. `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md` keeps the long-tail row PARTIAL.
